            <ArticleTitle>Knee osteoarthritis: Clinical connections to articular cartilage structure and function.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>301-16</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ptsp.2014.12.001</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1466-853X(14)00106-0</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Articular cartilage is a unique biphasic material that supports a lifetime of compressive and shear forces across joints. When articular cartilage deteriorates, whether due to injury, wear and tear or normal aging, osteoarthritis and resultant pain can ensue. Understanding the basic science of the structure and biomechanics of articular cartilage can help clinicians guide their patients to appropriate activity and loading choices. The purpose of this article is to examine how articular cartilage structure and mechanics, may interact with risk factors to contribute to OA and how this interaction provides guidelines for intervention choices This paper will review the microstructure of articular cartilage, its mechanical properties and link this information to clinical decision making.</AbstractText>
